  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 PM EDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery imagery reveals troughing stretching \ndown from the Hudson Bay down through the Great Lakes. Cold air \nadvection characterized by 850 mb temperatures falling into the \nsingle digits has been able to kick off scattered lake effect rain \nshowers for the NE UP. So far temperatures have only managed to peak \nin the low 60s to upper 50s this afternoon under gusty NW winds. \nTonight, lake effect showers dwindle in the east as drier air works \ninto the region. Under mostly clear skies, building high pressure, \nand diminishing winds, efficient radiational cooling will allow the \ninterior west to cool into the upper 20s to low 30s. Frost \nAdvisories have been expanded for most of the western UP save for \nthe Keweenaw. Elsewhere, patchy frost and upper 30s may be found \nacross the interior central and east, but confidence is lower on \nfrost potential there. Even the lakeshores may be able to enjoy a \ncool night in the 40s!\n\nOur attention then turns to a period of active weather tomorrow \nnight through Tuesday. High pressure overhead will keep the region \ndry through most of the day tomorrow, however, widespread rain is \nanticipated Monday night through Tuesday morning as a deep moves \nfrom the Northern Rockies into the High Plains. Moist return flow \n(PWATs climbing near the 95-99th percentile of climo) and strong \nisentropic ascent will be the focal point for a wave of showers and \nmaybe a few embedded thunderstorms moving west to east across the UP \nending early Tuesday. Ensembles still present some uncertainty \nregarding QPF amounts and where the greatest amounts will be found. \nThe tail end of this morning's HREF and REFS run suggest a 40-70% \nchance for rainfall amounts >0.5\" largely confined to the southern \nUP while global ensembles like the NBM and LREF are happy to paint \ngreater probs of >0.5\" (60-90%) across the entire region. \nRegardless, any meaningful rain is welcome to help curtail ongoing \ndrought. Strong southerly winds accompanying a 45-55kt LLJ and \npressure falls ramp up late Monday to early Tuesday. Widespread 30-\n40 mph wind gusts will be common. NBM and CAM guidance is a little \ntoo hot with the upper end of wind gust potential, suggesting \nfavorable probability for 40-45 kt gales on the lakes. Pattern \nrecognition tells us that this is not likely to happen, as mixing \ninto the 45-55 kt LLJ doesn't often occur given early fall over-lake \nstability. A few gusts >40 kts are possible, but widespread gusts \n>30-40 kts are hard to achieve in a southerly wind regime. What is \nmore likely to happen is strong westerlies on Lake Superior and in \nthe Keweenaw Tuesday afternoon as pressure rises (~1 mb/hr) and cold \nair advection contribute to gusts reaching 40-50 kts. Ensembles \nsuggest high probability (60-90% chance) for frequent gusts >45 mph \n(Wind Advisory criteria) in the Keweenaw. \n\nZonal flow and height rises contribute to drier and more seasonable \nweather heading into the mid to late week.",
